 07_8	What does the recipe do with a handful of just one box?
 07_9	It doubles the number and subtracts one. One box leaves two gaps, and the gap below is the only clue to the gap above. Box thirty-seven has thirty-six boxes below it, so the recipe says seventy-three.
 07_10	Nothing in the proof needed five. With any handful size, the numbers cut the unseen boxes into one more gap than there are numbers, all with the same average. So the recipe always adds the largest minus the size, over the size, and it's right on average for every count, as long as the handful is fair.
+08_1	Right on average is a promise about the recipe across all handfuls, not about our seventy-one. To see what it promises, imagine a company that made exactly seventy-one boxes, and friends who could buy five fresh boxes from it, again and again.
+08_2	Here are twenty such handfuls, drawn by computer.
+08_3	Their estimates land between about forty-five and eighty-four point two, and three of them land exactly on seventy-one. Together they average about seventy-two.
+08_4	Twenty is only twenty. This company has over thirteen million handfuls of five, and we can count them all, because the recipe looks only at the largest number. Just one handful tops out at five. Five handfuls top out at six. Over nine hundred thousand top out at seventy-one. So the handfuls fall into sixty-seven stacks by their largest number, and every handful in a stack gives the same estimate.
+08_5	Out of all those handfuls, how often do you think the estimate is exactly seventy-one, the true count?
+08_6	About one handful in twenty-nine. Three in twenty was lucky. Being right on average doesn't mean being right often.
+08_7	Yet all the bars together balance at seventy-one exactly, while our twenty draws averaged about seventy-two. The bars are lopsided, though. Fifty-eight percent of handfuls overestimate, by about eight boxes on average, and the estimates that fall short miss by twelve on average.
+08_8	So how far off is a typical estimate? Ignoring the direction, an estimate misses by about nine boxes on average. Statisticians often quote a different width, the standard deviation. It squares each miss before averaging, then takes the square root, so big misses count for more, and here it comes to about eleven point seven.
+08_9	So keep these ideas apart. The recipe is unbiased, so its estimates balance at the true count. They spread out around it, by about nine boxes on average. And our seventy-one is a single estimate, from a single handful.
+09_1	Back to the friends' own boxes. Their count is a fixed number nobody knows, at least sixty. How large could it be? Flip the question around. For each possible count, how often would a fair handful of five all be sixty or less?
+09_2	If the count were seventy-one, about forty-two percent of the time. If it were a hundred, about seven percent. The bigger the count, the rarer a handful this low.
+09_3	At a hundred and seven boxes the chance is still just over five percent. At a hundred and eight it's just under, and it keeps falling from there. Counts that big would rarely give a handful like ours.
+09_4	So keep every count from sixty to a hundred and seven, the counts that give a handful like ours at least five times in a hundred. That's our range.
+09_5	Is a range built this way any good? Test it where we know the answer. Fix the count at seventy-one, and let the twenty handfuls drawn by computer each build their own range the same way.
+09_6	Nineteen of the twenty ranges cover seventy-one. The range that misses came from a handful topped by thirty-eight. Over every handful, ranges built this way cover seventy-one about ninety-six times in a hundred, and whatever the count, never fewer than ninety-five.
+09_7	That's what ninety-five percent confidence means. The count stays fixed, and the range changes from handful to handful. It's a promise about the method, not a ninety-five percent chance that the count lies in our range.
+09_8	Behind each bar was a single division. Count the handfuls of five from the first sixty boxes, and divide by all the handfuls of five from the whole company. At a hundred and seven boxes, that's just over five percent.
+09_9	If the friends had ten boxes instead, with the same largest number, would the range get wider or narrower?
+09_10	Narrower. Ten numbers all sixty or less would be rare from a big company, so the range shrinks to sixty through seventy-nine.
